<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dissolution of Czechoslovakia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1993</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Velvet Divorce</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The dissolution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, known as the Velvet Divorce, divided the federation into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> on 1 January 1993. After the June 1992 elections, the Czech and Slovak leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaclav-klaus">Václav Klaus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-meciar">Vladimír Mečiar</a> agreed on separation, which the Federal Assembly approved in November without a referendum. The split was peaceful and negotiated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-czechoslovakia.jpg" alt="Dissolution of Czechoslovakia" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PANONIAN · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Czechoslovakia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
